1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,138
This is a hundred-dollar bill.

2
00:00:02,188 --> 00:00:04,714
It costs less than ten cents to make.

3
00:00:04,764 --> 00:00:09,518
And by the end of this video, this exact bill will make one man rich…

4
00:00:09,568 --> 00:00:10,980
and another man poor.

5
00:00:11,030 --> 00:00:13,556
Neither of them breaks a single rule.

6
00:00:13,606 --> 00:00:16,550
They both do exactly what they were taught.

7
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:17,626
Meet Jake.

8
00:00:17,676 --> 00:00:18,306
And Ryan.

9
00:00:18,356 --> 00:00:19,213
Same street.

10
00:00:19,263 --> 00:00:20,120
Same school.

11
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:21,027
Same grades.

12
00:00:21,077 --> 00:00:25,259
At twenty-five, they even get hired by the same company.

13
00:00:25,309 --> 00:00:26,015
Same desk.

14
00:00:26,065 --> 00:00:26,922
Same salary.

15
00:00:26,972 --> 00:00:29,189
Sixty thousand dollars a year.

16
00:00:29,559 --> 00:00:31,154
Twenty years later…

17
00:00:31,524 --> 00:00:33,104
…Ryan is a millionaire.

18
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:37,216
Jake is still renting, and still paying off a credit card.

19
00:00:37,266 --> 00:00:38,563
Ryan isn't smarter.

20
00:00:38,613 --> 00:00:40,478
He didn't inherit anything.

21
00:00:40,528 --> 00:00:41,896
He didn't get lucky.

22
00:00:41,946 --> 00:00:45,228
He just understood three things Jake never did.

23
00:00:45,278 --> 00:00:47,497
Where money actually comes from.

24
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:48,773
Who gets it first.

25
00:00:48,823 --> 00:00:52,389
And why the same paycheck can make one person rich…

26
00:00:52,439 --> 00:00:54,232
and keep another one poor.

27
00:00:54,282 --> 00:00:57,068
Once you see it, you can never unsee it.

28
00:01:02,038 --> 00:01:03,270
Let's follow the bill.

29
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:07,000
Jake gets his first paycheck, and he does the responsible thing.

30
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:08,399
He takes it to the bank.

31
00:01:08,769 --> 00:01:11,543
And here's the first thing nobody tells you.

32
00:01:11,593 --> 00:01:15,650
The moment that bill crosses the counter, it stops being Jake's.

33
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:18,603
Legally, Jake just lent his money to the bank.

34
00:01:18,653 --> 00:01:20,464
What he gets back isn't cash.

35
00:01:20,514 --> 00:01:22,004
It's a number in an app.

36
00:01:22,054 --> 00:01:22,646
A promise.

37
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:24,838
Think of it like a coat check.

38
00:01:24,888 --> 00:01:27,833
You hand over your coat and get a little ticket.

39
00:01:27,883 --> 00:01:30,703
Except this coat check doesn't keep your coat.

40
00:01:30,753 --> 00:01:35,508
It lends it out to someone else while you're inside, and charges them for it.

41
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:41,041
Before Jake is even home, the bank has lent his money to a stranger buying a car.

42
00:01:41,091 --> 00:01:42,714
At eight percent interest.

43
00:01:42,764 --> 00:01:44,387
Jake earns almost nothing.

44
00:01:44,437 --> 00:01:46,318
The bank keeps the difference.

45
00:01:46,688 --> 00:01:51,331
So how much does the bank actually have to keep in the vault, in case Jake wants

46
00:01:51,381 --> 00:01:52,211
his money back?

47
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:55,437
Since March 2020, in America, the legal requirement is…

48
00:01:55,487 --> 00:01:55,730
zero.

49
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:56,880
Zero percent.

50
00:01:56,930 --> 00:02:01,922
If everyone asked for their money on the same day, it simply wouldn't be there.

51
00:02:01,972 --> 00:02:06,837
That's exactly what happened in the 1930s, when thousands of banks collapsed.

52
00:02:06,887 --> 00:02:12,135
Today, the government insures deposits up to two hundred fifty thousand dollars, so

53
00:02:12,185 --> 00:02:13,220
people stay calm.

54
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:15,709
But that's not even the strangest part.

55
00:02:15,759 --> 00:02:19,028
The bank didn't even need Jake's bill to lend money.

56
00:02:19,078 --> 00:02:21,518
It can create money that never existed.

57
00:02:23,488 --> 00:02:25,882
Three years later, Ryan wants a house.

58
00:02:25,932 --> 00:02:29,292
He asks for a three-hundred-thousand-dollar mortgage.

59
00:02:29,662 --> 00:02:32,166
So where does that money come from?

60
00:02:32,216 --> 00:02:33,407
Not from a vault.

61
00:02:33,457 --> 00:02:35,888
Not from other customers' savings.

62
00:02:35,938 --> 00:02:37,567
The banker types it in.

63
00:02:37,617 --> 00:02:38,296
That's it.

64
00:02:38,346 --> 00:02:41,142
A second ago, that money did not exist.

65
00:02:41,192 --> 00:02:42,018
Now it does.

66
00:02:42,068 --> 00:02:45,959
And when Ryan pays the loan back, it disappears again.

67
00:02:46,329 --> 00:02:48,171
This isn't a conspiracy theory.

68
00:02:48,221 --> 00:02:50,674
The Bank of England published it in 2014.

69
00:02:50,724 --> 00:02:54,886
Most of the money in the modern economy is created by ordinary banks…

70
00:02:54,936 --> 00:02:56,473
whenever they make a loan.

71
00:02:56,843 --> 00:02:57,418
But wait.

72
00:02:57,468 --> 00:02:59,293
Isn't money backed by gold?

73
00:02:59,343 --> 00:03:00,265
It used to be.

74
00:03:00,315 --> 00:03:03,043
Until one Sunday evening in August 1971.

75
00:03:03,093 --> 00:03:08,806
President Nixon went on television and announced that the dollar would no longer be

76
00:03:08,856 --> 00:03:10,334
exchangeable for gold.

77
00:03:10,384 --> 00:03:14,778
Since that night, the dollar is backed by one thing only: trust.

78
00:03:14,828 --> 00:03:17,070
Trust that others will accept it.

79
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:22,070
Which means there's no natural limit on how many dollars can be created.

80
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:27,817
That's why only about one in ten dollars in America exists as physical cash.

81
00:03:27,867 --> 00:03:31,317
The rest is numbers on screens, created by loans.

82
00:03:31,687 --> 00:03:36,507
And above the banks sits the Federal Reserve, the bank of the banks.

83
00:03:36,557 --> 00:03:40,732
Between 2020 and 2022, it created trillions of new dollars.

84
00:03:40,782 --> 00:03:46,247
Its balance sheet more than doubled, from about four trillion to almost nine.

85
00:03:46,297 --> 00:03:48,825
Which leads to a dangerous question.

86
00:03:48,875 --> 00:03:51,116
When all that new money appears…

87
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:52,406
who gets it first?

88
00:03:54,376 --> 00:03:55,707
Picture a fountain.

89
00:03:55,757 --> 00:03:58,179
New money is poured in at the top.

90
00:03:58,229 --> 00:04:03,849
The people closest to it, banks, big investors, the already rich, drink first.

91
00:04:03,899 --> 00:04:06,539
By the time it trickles down to Jake…

92
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:07,920
it's barely a drop.

93
00:04:08,290 --> 00:04:13,817
An economist named Richard Cantillon noticed this almost three hundred years ago.

94
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:17,740
Whoever gets new money first spends it at the old prices.

95
00:04:17,790 --> 00:04:19,186
Whoever gets it last…

96
00:04:19,236 --> 00:04:20,425
pays the new ones.

97
00:04:20,795 --> 00:04:25,979
In 2020, the amount of dollars in America grew by about twenty-five percent.

98
00:04:26,029 --> 00:04:27,150
In a single year.

99
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,180
The money went into stocks and houses first.

100
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:34,656
American home prices jumped by around forty percent in two years.

101
00:04:34,706 --> 00:04:38,926
American billionaires got more than a trillion dollars richer.

102
00:04:39,296 --> 00:04:39,967
And Jake?

103
00:04:40,017 --> 00:04:41,812
Jake got a small raise.

104
00:04:41,862 --> 00:04:44,538
And much more expensive groceries.

105
00:04:44,588 --> 00:04:48,467
Which brings us to the tax nobody ever voted for.

106
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:50,388
Here's the cruel part.

107
00:04:50,438 --> 00:04:55,190
Jake's boss gives him a three-percent raise, and Jake is grateful.

108
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:59,119
The same month, his landlord raises the rent by eight.

109
00:04:59,169 --> 00:05:01,739
On paper, Jake earns more than ever.

110
00:05:01,789 --> 00:05:05,086
In reality, he can afford less than last year.

111
00:05:05,136 --> 00:05:09,161
Wages are always the last thing in line at the fountain.

112
00:05:11,131 --> 00:05:13,468
This is what inflation really does.

113
00:05:13,518 --> 00:05:18,787
A hundred dollars from the year 2000 buys about what fifty-five did back then.

114
00:05:18,837 --> 00:05:21,105
Nobody took it out of your wallet.

115
00:05:21,155 --> 00:05:21,651
It just…

116
00:05:21,701 --> 00:05:23,219
quietly lost its power.

117
00:05:23,589 --> 00:05:28,679
Jake keeps his savings in the bank, earning less than half a percent.

118
00:05:28,729 --> 00:05:31,584
Prices rise about three percent a year.

119
00:05:31,634 --> 00:05:33,669
So Jake isn't really saving.

120
00:05:33,719 --> 00:05:34,563
He's losing.

121
00:05:34,613 --> 00:05:35,085
Slowly.

122
00:05:35,135 --> 00:05:36,425
Every single year.

123
00:05:36,795 --> 00:05:38,661
Remember the three questions?

124
00:05:38,711 --> 00:05:42,493
We now know where money comes from, and who gets it first.

125
00:05:42,543 --> 00:05:45,202
But the most important one is still open.

126
00:05:45,252 --> 00:05:49,430
Why did the same paycheck take these two in opposite directions?

127
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:51,808
It started with one boring decision.

128
00:05:52,178 --> 00:05:54,590
Ryan did something boring at twenty-five.

129
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:59,572
Every month, before he spent anything, five hundred dollars went automatically into

130
00:05:59,622 --> 00:06:00,413
an index fund.

131
00:06:00,463 --> 00:06:04,134
A tiny piece of the five hundred biggest companies in America.

132
00:06:04,504 --> 00:06:09,651
Over the long run, the stock market has grown about ten percent a year on average.

133
00:06:09,701 --> 00:06:12,375
Not guaranteed, with crashes along the way.

134
00:06:12,425 --> 00:06:13,896
But look what time does.

135
00:06:13,946 --> 00:06:18,776
The same ten thousand dollars, left alone for twenty years: in Jake's savings

136
00:06:18,826 --> 00:06:21,437
account, about ten thousand eight hundred.

137
00:06:21,487 --> 00:06:25,112
In Ryan's fund, historically, around sixty-seven thousand.

138
00:06:25,483 --> 00:06:27,878
Meanwhile, Jake did what felt normal.

139
00:06:27,928 --> 00:06:28,538
A new car.

140
00:06:28,588 --> 00:06:29,199
On a loan.

141
00:06:29,249 --> 00:06:33,891
The moment he drove it off the lot, it lost about a fifth of its value.

142
00:06:33,941 --> 00:06:36,336
Ryan's money bought things that grow.

143
00:06:36,386 --> 00:06:38,914
Jake's money bought things that shrink.

144
00:06:38,964 --> 00:06:41,425
And Jake's real trap was still coming.

145
00:06:43,395 --> 00:06:45,292
One day, a letter arrives.

146
00:06:45,342 --> 00:06:46,490
Congratulations.

147
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,062
Jake is pre-approved.

148
00:06:48,112 --> 00:06:50,383
Ten thousand dollars of credit.

149
00:06:50,753 --> 00:06:52,090
Now look at the deal.

150
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:55,987
The bank pays Jake less than half a percent on his savings.

151
00:06:56,037 --> 00:06:59,157
And charges him over twenty percent on his card.

152
00:06:59,207 --> 00:07:01,468
With money it can create by typing.

153
00:07:01,518 --> 00:07:04,705
Jake is on the losing side of every single trade.

154
00:07:05,075 --> 00:07:10,566
A five-thousand-dollar balance, paid back only at the minimum, can take more than

155
00:07:10,616 --> 00:07:11,250
ten years…

156
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,371
and cost thousands in interest.

157
00:07:13,421 --> 00:07:15,355
It's not that Jake is stupid.

158
00:07:15,405 --> 00:07:19,802
Nobody ever showed him which side of the table he was sitting on.

159
00:07:19,852 --> 00:07:21,034
And the very rich?

160
00:07:21,084 --> 00:07:25,618
They sit on a side of the table most people don't even know exists.

161
00:07:27,588 --> 00:07:29,380
Imagine you own a billion dollars in stock.

162
00:07:29,430 --> 00:07:30,580
If you sell it, you pay tax.

163
00:07:30,630 --> 00:07:30,709
So…

164
00:07:30,759 --> 00:07:31,351
you don't sell.

165
00:07:31,721 --> 00:07:34,757
Instead, you borrow against your shares.

166
00:07:34,807 --> 00:07:36,299
A loan isn't income.

167
00:07:36,349 --> 00:07:38,228
No income, no income tax.

168
00:07:38,278 --> 00:07:42,162
Your stocks keep growing, faster than the interest.

169
00:07:42,212 --> 00:07:43,704
So you borrow again.

170
00:07:43,754 --> 00:07:47,947
Reporters at ProPublica gave this strategy a name: Buy.

171
00:07:47,997 --> 00:07:48,487
Borrow.

172
00:07:48,537 --> 00:07:48,795
Die.

173
00:07:49,165 --> 00:07:50,741
Ryan isn't a billionaire.

174
00:07:50,791 --> 00:07:53,734
But he plays a small version of the same game.

175
00:07:53,784 --> 00:07:55,946
His loan bought a house that grew.

176
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:58,353
His paycheck bought shares that grew.

177
00:07:58,403 --> 00:08:01,085
Jake's loans bought a car and a lifestyle.

178
00:08:01,135 --> 00:08:03,297
And when the biggest players lose…

179
00:08:03,347 --> 00:08:05,379
something even stranger happens.

180
00:08:07,349 --> 00:08:12,682
In 2008, the biggest banks made reckless bets with borrowed money, and lost.

181
00:08:12,732 --> 00:08:14,736
They weren't allowed to fail.

182
00:08:14,786 --> 00:08:20,543
Seven hundred billion dollars of taxpayer money was approved to rescue the system.

183
00:08:20,913 --> 00:08:23,365
In 2023, Silicon Valley Bank collapsed.

184
00:08:23,415 --> 00:08:28,498
Every depositor got their money back, even the ones far above the insured limit.

185
00:08:28,548 --> 00:08:30,872
When they win, they keep the profits.

186
00:08:30,922 --> 00:08:33,631
When they lose, everyone else catches them.

187
00:08:33,681 --> 00:08:36,582
Remember Jake's credit card at twenty percent?

188
00:08:36,632 --> 00:08:38,635
Nobody is coming to rescue that.

189
00:08:39,005 --> 00:08:43,565
And every time there's a crisis, the Federal Reserve does the same thing.

190
00:08:43,615 --> 00:08:48,490
It cuts interest rates, sometimes to zero, and creates new money to buy bonds.

191
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:51,395
That pushes up the price of stocks and houses.

192
00:08:51,445 --> 00:08:54,741
So the people who already own them are rescued twice.

193
00:08:54,791 --> 00:08:56,067
First by the bailout.

194
00:08:56,117 --> 00:08:57,456
Then by the new money.

195
00:08:57,826 --> 00:09:00,082
So let's answer the three questions.

196
00:09:00,132 --> 00:09:01,811
Where does money come from?

197
00:09:01,861 --> 00:09:04,372
Mostly from loans, typed into existence.

198
00:09:04,422 --> 00:09:05,525
Who gets it first?

199
00:09:05,575 --> 00:09:07,254
Those closest to the banks.

200
00:09:07,304 --> 00:09:10,584
And why does the same paycheck make one person rich…

201
00:09:10,634 --> 00:09:11,993
and keep another poor?

202
00:09:12,043 --> 00:09:14,619
Let's go back to our hundred-dollar bill.

203
00:09:16,589 --> 00:09:17,527
The same bill.

204
00:09:17,577 --> 00:09:23,386
In Jake's hands, it's something to spend, or to park in an account that slowly eats

205
00:09:23,436 --> 00:09:23,598
it.

206
00:09:23,648 --> 00:09:25,645
In Ryan's hands, it's a tool.

207
00:09:25,695 --> 00:09:29,740
A tiny piece of things that grow while the dollar shrinks.

208
00:09:30,110 --> 00:09:31,457
That's the whole secret.

209
00:09:31,507 --> 00:09:33,959
Own things that grow when money is printed.

210
00:09:34,009 --> 00:09:36,986
Stay away from debt that charges you twenty percent.

211
00:09:37,036 --> 00:09:41,701
And don't let your long-term savings sit somewhere that pays less than inflation.

212
00:09:41,751 --> 00:09:43,331
This isn't financial advice.

213
00:09:43,381 --> 00:09:45,252
It's just how the game is played.

214
00:09:45,622 --> 00:09:48,220
Jake can't get those twenty years back.

215
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:50,326
But he finally knows the rules.

216
00:09:50,696 --> 00:09:51,547
So Jake starts.

217
00:09:51,597 --> 00:09:52,750
Late, but he starts.

218
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,373
He pays off the card first.

219
00:09:54,423 --> 00:09:59,241
Then five hundred dollars a month, automatically, into the same kind of fund Ryan

220
00:09:59,291 --> 00:09:59,602
chose.

221
00:09:59,652 --> 00:10:04,471
If the market does what it has done historically, and nothing is guaranteed, that

222
00:10:04,521 --> 00:10:09,340
could grow to over three hundred thousand dollars by the time Jake is sixty-five.

223
00:10:09,390 --> 00:10:10,963
Not because he got smarter.

224
00:10:11,013 --> 00:10:14,329
Because he finally moved to the other side of the table.

225
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:15,659
Same salary.

226
00:10:15,709 --> 00:10:16,669
Same street.

227
00:10:16,719 --> 00:10:18,016
Different rules.

228
00:10:18,066 --> 00:10:20,121
Money was never the game.

229
00:10:20,171 --> 00:10:21,889
Knowing the rules is.

230
00:10:22,259 --> 00:10:24,902
If this changed how you see money, subscribe.

231
00:10:24,952 --> 00:10:27,833
There's a lot more the banks will never tell you.
